    <string name="accessibility_service_summary">Lock the screen and pull down the notifications from the home screen</string>
    <string name="accessibility_service_description">Alisio uses this service for two things only: locking the screen when you double tap its home screen, and pulling down the notifications when you swipe down on it. It does not read what is on the screen, it receives no events and it sends nothing anywhere.</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_title">Before you turn it on</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_does_label">What it does</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_does_lock">Locks the screen when you double tap an empty area of the home screen</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_does_notifications">Pulls down the notifications when you swipe down on the home screen</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_does_not_label">What it doesn\'t do</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_does_not">It doesn\'t read the screen or what you type, or tap anything for you. No Internet.</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_why">It doesn\'t watch other apps: it asks Android for no events and no window content, and Alisio has no Internet permission. Android only lets an accessibility service lock the screen or open the notifications. Without it those two gestures do nothing, and the rest of Alisio works the same. Some apps, such as banking apps, check for active accessibility services and may warn you.</string>
    <string name="accessibility_notice_how">On the next screen, find Alisio (on Samsung, under Installed apps) and turn it on. Android shows its own generic warning about full control: it is the same for every service. You can turn it off there at any time.</string>
